# uart: a transmitter and a receiver behind one baud divisor. The package
# ships eight modules in two layers. `top` names `uart`, which is the
# 8N1-by-default pair a design with a fixed framing wants, and a design
# that wants only one half instantiates `uart_tx` or `uart_rx` directly.
# Underneath them `uart_frame`, `uart_frame_tx` and `uart_frame_rx` are
# the same hardware with the character format on **ports** instead of
# parameters, which is what a USB serial port needs: `uart_baud_div`
# turns a bit rate into the `div` every one of them takes, and
# `uart_line_coding` turns a host's SET_LINE_CODING into the format the
# frame modules take — so that a host's `stty` reaches the wire and not
# just a register. README.md says which to use and what each costs.
name uart
version 1.0.0
license MIT
description "UART transmitter and receiver with a ready/valid interface, selectable data bits, parity and stop bits, distinguished receive errors, and a run-time or parameterised baud divisor"

top uart
target ice40
target ecp5

source rtl/uart_frame_tx.v
source rtl/uart_frame_rx.v
source rtl/uart_frame.v
source rtl/uart_tx.v
source rtl/uart_rx.v
source rtl/uart.v
source rtl/uart_baud_div.v
source rtl/uart_line_coding.v

param CLK_DIV int 16 4..65535
param DATA_BITS int 8 5..8
param PARITY int 0 0..4
param STOP_BITS int 1 1..2

port clk in
port rst_n in
port div in 16
port tx_data in 8
port tx_valid in
port tx_ready out
port tx out
port rx in
port rx_data out 8
port rx_valid out
port rx_error out
port rx_frame_error out
port rx_parity_error out
port rx_break out
